ey try to do that now with marvel and  i hope they keep doing that forever  hey you guys make love not war  my publisher had learned that dc  comics which was then called national  comics  had a book called the justice league and  it was selling very well  and it was made up of a number of  superheroes so he said to me stan  why don't you come up with a book with a  team of superheroes  and i really didn't want to do the same  old thing  with guys who just wear costumes and  masks and have secret identities  and and the girl is always a token  girl who has to be rescued all the time  in fact at that time i really wanted to  quit because i had been doing these  comics for about  20 years and they were  i had the same instructions for my  publisher all the time  don't use words of more than two  syllables  don't play up too much dialogue the  readers get bored reading dialogue and  so i felt i was just writing  one-dimensional stories  and my wife said to me you know stan if  you want to quit anyway  why don't you do the next book you do  the way you want to do it just for fun  the worst that happens is you'll be  fired  and you want to quit so what's the big  deal  and luckily that was when he asked me to  do this  team of superheroes so i thought how can  i make them a little different than  what had gone before one  no secret identity two the girl  is gonna be as much a valuable member of  the team as the other three guys  three i am  instead of the heroine not knowing that  the hero  is really a hero you know the clark  can't lose  lane's syndrome i'll let them be engaged  they know each other and they know what  they do and that's  it how do you how do you keep the  creative juices flowing  to keep creating work over the years  greed  is probably one of the reasons no  actually  i'm doing what i enjoy doing it's like  other men like to play golf  so they play golf every chance they can  so you don't say to him how come you're  playing golf today you played last week  was the same with me to me it is such  fun creating characters writing stories  even doing interviews even though i  can't hear most of what the guy says to  me  but it's an exciting life  and when you do something that you know  the fans seem to  enjoy that gives you such satisfaction  you don't want to stop  there was a character and it was owned  by fawcett and by disney there was  fighting over it  and we couldn't use the name but i  wanted  our name was marvel comics and i wanted  something with the word marvel in it  and we finally decided we made her a  female  i don't know i just decided to make her  a female  i can't remember why i thought  i like things that are different if you  do what everybody expects  you're just doing the ordinary but if  you surprise  people that's fun and i think people  were surprised to see that our captain  marvel was a female  so that was fun for me you know that is  probably the toughest question  i could ever think of to answer because  i don't  know what what i would do was just sit  back and try to think of something  i don't know that there's any  inspiration involved if  when i worked at marvel i worked for a  publisher  and for example when the fantastic force  sold  well he said oh maybe the fans like  groups of heroes come up with another  group  so i sat and i thought and i came up  with the x-men  but how i did it or i don't know i mean  i just tried to think of a group  and i probably thought of a number of  them where i said nah that's no good now  that's no good  in fact with the x-men i remember one of  the toughest things is coming up with  the super power how do they get their  superpower  and i had already had the fantastic four  get their power from cosmic rays  and i think daredevil got his from gamma  rays  and spider-man got his from a  radioactive spider and i was running out  of ideas  so i said hey i'll make a mutants  they're born that way and then i don't  need any new rays  but it's just a matter of thinking i  mean  you you sit and you think until the  right idea hits you right  there's no trick to it  you


